        "geology": "The area began to develop in the postglacial period when the Earth crust rose and the sea gradually stepped back. The parent material, and the soils themselves, have been formed under the strong influence of coastal processes.",
        "hydrology": "The subsoil water may reach the upper layer of soil. During heavy rains and storms, floods are common. Recent increased precipitation during warmer winters has promoted the water saturation of the meadows.",
        "soils": "The soils are composed of various sorted material: fine sands and gravel to boulders. The soils are classified as seaside sandy marsh soils. In topographical depressions the organic layer is thicker, reaching 30 cm. Soluble potassium and phosphorus concentrations are relatively high, and buried soils are frequent",
        "vegetation": "The ?Randu Meadows? are an unique mosaic of local depressions, lagoons and man-made ditches, interspersed with elevated dunes, leading to high spatial heterogeneity of vegetation. Communities of hydrophylous and hygrophylous species are found side by side with xerophylous meadow communities and dune vegetation. This diversity of habitats causes high species diversity. At present, 531 plant species have been found in this relatively small area; of which 36 plant species are protected by the Red Data Book of Latvia."
      },
